NOTES = {
"title": "This chapter of the book is about making pictures and graphs with a program. It has fifteen examples, and all of them run on the chapter page in the order of this deck, with no picture file to download and no window to open: every picture is drawn by the example itself. Parts 1 and 2 change pictures with Pillow, part 3 adds a logo and draws shapes and text, part 4 covers the clipboard and Matplotlib graphs, and part 5 combines them in practice programs. Slide 2 shows the route through the five parts.",
"route": "The chapter has five parts. Part 1 explains pixels, colours, coordinates, cropping and pasting. Part 2 changes pictures: masks, resizing, turning, single pixels and saving in two formats. Part 3 is the logo project and then drawing shapes and text. Part 4 puts a picture on the clipboard and draws graphs with Matplotlib. Part 5 has the practice programs: a tile sheet and seating cards. The right-hand column lists the examples of each part, and the last row points to the summary and the links at the end.",
"questions": "Two questions frame the chapter. The first asks how a program changes a picture without damaging the original, and how it names the pixels it changes. The answer is a new Image from each call and a box of coordinates. The second asks how a program turns numbers into a graph or a card, and what it must do first so that it runs with no window: it chooses a backend that draws into memory. Slide 39 collects the claims, and slide 38 has practice questions.",
"divider1": "Part 1 is about the grid of pixels. Each pixel holds four numbers for red, green, blue and alpha, and each position is a pair of numbers counted from the top left. The part names colours with ImageColor, cuts out a region with a box tuple, crops a new Image and pastes one picture onto another in two loops. Slide 5 explains the concepts in plain words, and slide 7 tells how the first scanned photograph was made. The table lists the slides of the part.",
"divider2": "Part 2 changes pictures in more ways. It starts with the alpha channel and the mask of paste(), which decides how much of the top picture shows. It then resizes, rotates and flips, sets and reads single pixels, and saves a picture in PNG and in JPEG. The stories explain where alpha, PNG and JPEG came from. Slide 14 explains the concepts of the part, and the table lists its slides.",
"divider3": "Part 3 has the first project of the chapter and then drawing. The project puts a logo on every picture of a folder: it opens the logo, loops over the files, shrinks the large pictures and pastes the logo into a corner. After it the part draws points, lines, rectangles, ellipses and polygons with an ImageDraw object, and measures and draws text. Slides 22 and 23 explain the concepts, and slide 24 tells the story of the library itself.",
"divider4": "Part 4 starts with the clipboard, where a picture can wait between two programs, and then draws graphs. Matplotlib turns lists of numbers into line graphs, scatter plots, bar graphs and pie charts, and its pyplot module keeps one current figure that every call draws on. The part also shows why the backend matters: one that draws into memory needs no window. Slide 29 explains the concepts, and slide 30 tells how Matplotlib got its name.",
"divider5": "Part 5 has the practice programs that combine the earlier parts. Tile Maker pastes one picture across and down to make a larger sheet. The seating-card program draws one card of 288 by 360 pixels for each guest name. The third program of the book looks for folders of photographs on a disk, and the questions after the examples use it. Slide 35 explains the concepts and the files that the programs use.",
"A1": "Every picture that a program handles is a grid of pixels. A pixel holds four numbers from 0 to 255 for red, green, blue and alpha, and alpha 255 means solid. A position is a pair counted from the top left corner, so x grows to the right and y grows downward. A box tuple is left, top, right and bottom, and the last two lie just outside the box. ImageColor turns a name such as CornflowerBlue or a code such as #CCDDEE into these numbers. Slide 8 runs the first example.",
"A2": "Pillow opens, changes and saves pictures through one type, Image, which lives in the module PIL.Image. Image.open() reads a file, Image.new() makes a blank picture, and size, width and height describe it. crop() returns a new Image of a box and leaves the original alone, while paste() puts one picture onto another and changes the target. The loop of the book's figure pastes the same face at every position. Slides 11 and 12 run these two calls.",
"A3": "Each of these calls returns a new Image except paste() and putpixel(), which change the picture they are called on. resize() takes the new size. rotate() turns the picture, and the corners are cut off unless expand=True makes the canvas larger. transpose() flips it. getpixel() reads one pixel and putpixel() sets one. The third argument of paste() is a mask: with it the alpha channel blends the two pictures, and without it the pixels are replaced. Slide 15 tells where alpha came from.",
"A4": "Project 16 puts the same logo on every picture of a folder. Step 1 opens the logo once and keeps its size. Step 2 loops over the file names that os.listdir() returns and skips anything that is not a picture. Step 3 shrinks a large picture to 300 pixels at most, keeping the proportion of its sides. Step 4 pastes the logo into the bottom right corner, using the logo itself as the mask, and saves the result. Ideas for similar programs are text, borders and format conversion. Slide 25 runs the steps.",
"A5": "An ImageDraw object draws on the Image it was made from. Its methods are point(), line(), rectangle(), ellipse(), polygon() and text(). A rectangle includes its right and bottom pixels, so a box of 20, 30, 60, 60 covers 41 by 31 pixels. An ellipse is the oval that fits the box, and a polygon joins its corners and closes the shape. ImageFont loads a font, and textbbox() measures a string before it is drawn. The book's drawing example saves all of the shapes as drawing.png. Slides 26 and 27 run the code.",
"A6": "pyperclipimg moves a picture through the clipboard with copy() and paste(), and it needs a display to do so. Matplotlib draws graphs from lists of numbers, and its pyplot module has plot(), scatter(), bar(), pie() and savefig(). A line graph joins its points and a scatter plot only marks them. Bar graphs draw one bar for each value and pie charts one wedge. A title, axis labels and a legend are additional components, each added by its own call. Slide 31 runs the clipboard example.",
"A7": "The three practice programs combine the earlier parts. Tile Maker pastes one picture, the cat picture of the book, across and down. The seating-card program reads guest names from a text file with one name on each line and draws one card of 4 by 5 inches for each, at 72 pixels per inch. The third program walks the folders of a disk and calls a folder a photo folder when most of its pictures are large. Slides 36 and 37 run the first two.",
"colours": "The first printed lines give each name the numbers that ImageColor returns: red is 255, 0, 0, 255 and CornflowerBlue is 100, 149, 237, 255. The code #CCDDEE is a different way to write 204, 221, 238. The last line shows that a picture of mode 1 and size 176 by 176 holds 30976 pixels, the size of the first scanned photograph on slide 7. Every colour is four numbers, and getcolor() returns them for any name. Slide 9 lists seven names and their numbers.",
"compare_colours": "The table lists seven colour names and what ImageColor.getcolor() returned for each: the four numbers, the same colour as a hex code, and which of red, green and blue is the largest part. Yellow is 255, 255, 0 and orange is 255, 165, 0, so both are mostly red and green. The alpha number is 255 in every row, which means solid. The book's Table 21-1 is a short list of such names. ImageColor knows many more, and an unknown name raises an error. Slide 10 turns to coordinates.",
"boxtuple": "The first printed line gives the size of a picture, 28 by 27, with its width and height. The second says that the box (3, 1, 9, 6) is 6 wide and 5 high, because the right and bottom values are subtracted from, and not part of, the box. The third line shows that the crop is 6 by 5 and the picture is still 28 by 27. The origin is the top left, x grows to the right and y downward. The box keeps its left and top and stops before its right and bottom. Slide 11 crops.",
"crop": "The first printed line shows that the original is still 200 by 140 and the face is 81 by 81. The second line shows that both pixels read are black: the middle of the dark face in the original, and the middle of the cut-out. crop() returns a new Image, so nothing is lost from the original, and the box (60, 30, 141, 111) is 81 wide because its right and bottom lie outside. The picture is drawn by the example itself, so no file is needed. Slide 12 pastes.",
"paste": "The first printed line says 15 pastes: the sheet of 200 by 120 holds 5 faces across and 3 down, each 40 pixels. The second line shows an orange pixel in the last corner and None for the value that paste() returned. paste() changes the picture it is called on and returns nothing, so a program that writes sheet = sheet.paste(...) loses the sheet. Copy the target first if the original is needed. Two loops over left and top do the tiling. Slide 13 begins part 2.",
"mask": "The first printed line is the pixel pasted without a mask: 0, 0, 255, 128, the blue square replaced the red base completely. The second line is the pixel pasted with the square as its own mask: 127, 0, 128, 191, a blend of red and blue whose alpha is 191. The mask tells paste() how much of the top picture to use at each pixel, and the alpha channel of the square itself serves as that mask. Slide 15 tells where alpha came from. Slide 17 resizes and turns.",
"transform": "The first line shows resize() giving 60 by 40, rotate(90) keeping 120 by 80, and rotate(90, expand=True) giving 80 by 120. The second line shows that a turn of 6 degrees keeps the size unless expand=True, which grows the canvas to 128 by 94. The third line is the flip, still 120 by 80. The fourth line shows the pixel set to red and its neighbour still white. The last line shows black in the corner that the turn left empty. Slide 18 tells the story of PNG.",
"save": "The first two printed lines give the sizes in bytes of the same picture saved as PNG, 1219, and as JPEG, 3648: for flat colours PNG is smaller, while for photographs JPEG usually wins because it throws detail away. The last line is the error Pillow raises when an RGBA picture is saved as JPEG: cannot write mode RGBA as JPEG. JPEG has no alpha channel, so convert the picture to RGB first. The pictures are saved into memory, not into files. Slide 21 begins part 3.",
"logo": "The printed lines show the two pictures that were stamped: 300.png and 500.png, both 300 by 150 after shrinking. The third picture, 80.png, printed None and was skipped because it was narrower than four logos. The program has four small steps: open the logo, test whether a picture is large enough, shrink it to 300 pixels at most with its proportions kept, and paste the logo into the bottom right corner with its own alpha as the mask. The files are made by the example in a temporary folder. Slide 26 draws shapes.",
"draw": "The printed line says 1271 blue pixels. The rectangle drawn from 20, 30 to 60, 60 includes its right and bottom pixels, so it is 41 wide and 31 high, and 41 times 31 is 1271; a program that counted 40 by 30 would expect 1200. The example also draws a point, a line of width 3, an outline of an ellipse and a filled polygon. Counting colours with getcolors() reads the result back from the picture itself. Slide 27 adds text.",
"text": "The first printed line is the box of the text Howdy!: 10, 17, 93, 39. The second line gives its size, 83 wide and 22 high, so a program can place the text before it draws it. The last line, True, says that the picture now holds more than one colour, so text() drew something. The font is the one that Pillow carries itself, because a font file such as arial.ttf is not on every computer. Slide 28 begins part 4.",
"clipboard": "The two printed values are the size of the picture that came back from the clipboard, 30 by 20, and its first pixel, orange. The real pyperclipimg needs a display, which a server or a page does not have, so the example marks itself EMULATED and uses a small class with the same two calls: copy() keeps a picture and paste() hands it back. Keep the difference in mind: the stand-in shows the shape of the calls, not the operating system's clipboard. Slide 32 draws graphs.",
"line": "The printed line shows four things: 1 for the number of lines plot() returned, PathCollection for what scatter() returned, Growth for the title that was set, and True because savefig() wrote more than a thousand bytes. The Agg backend draws into memory, so no window opens. plot() and scatter() draw on the current axes, and the title, the labels and the legend are further calls on the same figure. Save with savefig() before any call to show(). Slide 33 draws bar graphs and pie charts.",
"barpie": "The first printed line is the heights of the three bars, 5, 3 and 2. The second line says that pie() returned 3 wedges and that the first spans 180 degrees, because 5 of the 10 votes belong to A. bar() returns one bar for each value and pie() one wedge for each share. plt.figure() starts a new graph, so the pie is not drawn over the bars. The values are typed into the example, so the run never changes. Slide 34 begins part 5.",
"tile": "The printed line shows the sheet size, 200 by 120, and two pixels read at the middle of two different tiles, both black. The tile is 50 by 40, so 4 across and 3 down make 200 by 120. make_tile() makes a blank sheet and pastes the face at every position, computed as x times the width and y times the height. The book's version opens the cat picture from a file; here the face is drawn, so the example needs no file. Slide 37 draws seating cards.",
"seating": "The first printed line says 3 cards of 288 by 360 pixels, which is 4 by 5 inches at 72 pixels per inch. The second line shows a white corner and a middle that is not white, so the name was drawn. Each name is centred by the width that textbbox() measures. The book reads the names from a text file, guests.txt; here the list is typed in. The photo-folder program of the book is described on slide 35 and in the practice questions. Slide 38 has the questions.",
"practice": "These are the practice questions, and each one points to the part where the idea was shown. The first is about the box tuple, slide 10. The second is about copy() and the return value of paste(), slide 12. The third is about the mask, slide 16. The fourth is about JPEG and the alpha channel, slide 20. The fifth is about saving and showing a graph, slide 32. Run the examples on the chapter page to check each answer, and change a number to see what changes.",
"summary": "Four sentences carry the chapter. A picture is a grid of pixels, and a box tuple names a region of it. crop(), resize() and rotate() return new pictures, while paste() changes its target. A mask lets alpha blend a paste, JPEG has no alpha and PNG keeps it. pyplot draws on one current figure, and savefig() comes before show(). They answer the two questions of slide 3: change pictures without damage, and draw with no window. Slide 40 lists the links.",
"resources": "These fifteen links are where to go next. They include the chapter of the book, the documentation of Pillow and of Matplotlib, and the pages behind the stories: the first scanned photograph, the alpha channel, PNG, JPEG, the Python Imaging Library and Matplotlib. The two package pages show the current releases. Every link was opened on the day this deck was made. Read the documentation page of a method before you use it, because Pillow removes old methods and changes behaviour between releases.",
}

STORY_NOTES = {
"kirsch": "In 1957 Russell A. Kirsch and his group at the National Bureau of Standards built a drum scanner for the computer SEAC. One of the first pictures it scanned showed his three-month-old son, Walden: 176 by 176 pixels, 30976 in all, with one bit for each pixel, so only black or white. The picture of this slide is the one on the page of the source. The lesson: every picture you open began as a grid of numbers. Slide 8 makes a picture of mode 1 and the same size.",
"alpha": "In the late 1970s Alvy Ray Smith and Ed Catmull introduced the alpha channel at the Computer Graphics Lab of the New York Institute of Technology. A paper of 1984 by Thomas Porter and Tom Duff introduced premultiplied alpha. The fourth number of an RGBA tuple in this chapter is that alpha, from 0 for clear to 255 for solid. The lesson: alpha says how much of the top picture you see. Slide 16 pastes with and without a mask.",
"png": "On 28 December 1994 the announcement that GIF programs would owe royalties to Unisys for the LZW compression started the work on a free format. Thomas Boutell posted a plan on comp.graphics on 4 January 1995, and PNG was first released on 1 October 1996. PNG keeps every pixel and the alpha channel, which the logo project needs. The lesson: a format can be born from a licence dispute. Slide 19 tells the story of JPEG, and slide 20 saves in both formats.",
"jpeg": "In 1972 Nasir Ahmed proposed the discrete cosine transform for image compression, and the Joint Photographic Experts Group built its lossy standard on it in 1992. The standard is ISO/IEC 10918, also called ITU-T T.81. The lesson: JPEG throws detail away to make a small file, and it has no alpha channel, which is why Pillow refuses to save an RGBA picture as JPEG. Slide 20 shows the refusal and the two file sizes.",
"pil": "Fredrik Lundh wrote the Python Imaging Library, released in 1995 at Secret Labs AB. Development stopped in 2011, and the Pillow project forked it and added Python 3 support. Chapter 21 still imports from PIL, because Pillow kept the name of the package. The lesson: install Pillow, import PIL, and read the release notes, because methods such as textsize() have been removed. Slide 25 runs the first project of the chapter.",
"matplotlib": "John D. Hunter wrote Matplotlib and released it in 2003, as a plotting library for Python and its numerical extension NumPy. The name joins MATLAB, plot and library. The pyplot module keeps one current figure that each call draws on, so plt.plot() needs no figure object. The lesson: pyplot remembers the current figure, so you do not have to. Slide 31 starts with the clipboard, and slide 32 draws graphs.",
}

